1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,396
Most teams decide what to automate from a hunch, a vendor demo, or the loudest person in the room.

2
00:00:07,396 --> 00:00:12,000
There is a better place to start: the people who do the work.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
You set up a discovery campaign from a template, write a short welcome, and share one link, or a QR code, with your team.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,821
Your colleagues open it on a laptop or a phone.

5
00:00:28,821 --> 00:00:29,949
No account.

6
00:00:29,949 --> 00:00:40,000
They see who is asking and why, what is kept, and how to delete it, before they agree to anything.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,179
Then an AI interviewer asks about their actual week.

8
00:00:44,179 --> 00:00:46,027
One question at a time.

9
00:00:46,027 --> 00:00:53,741
It follows the same template for everyone, and it is instructed never to ask where AI should go.

10
00:00:53,741 --> 00:00:58,000
It asks what fills the day, and what gets in the way.

11
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:08,000
They can type, tap an answer option, or simply record an answer, where voice is set up.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,063
At the end, they see a summary they can correct.

13
00:01:14,063 --> 00:01:20,000
They can download what they said, or delete it.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,095
Now the part a chat window cannot give you.

15
00:01:24,095 --> 00:01:35,048
Every interview is turned into items on a fixed schema, then grouped, counted and scored across the whole campaign.

16
00:01:35,048 --> 00:01:40,000
In this demo: six simulated colleagues, nine groups.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,012
Each opportunity is scored on seven factors, with the reasons.

18
00:01:46,012 --> 00:01:49,406
And sometimes the answer is not AI.

19
00:01:49,406 --> 00:01:53,867
Here it says why plain automation fits better.

20
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:56,000
That is a result, too.

21
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,531
Departments are compared only when at least three people, the default, are behind a number.

22
00:02:04,531 --> 00:02:07,063
Below that, it is withheld.

23
00:02:07,063 --> 00:02:08,000
By design.

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:20,000
The report states its method, and what it does not do, and exports as a PDF you can hand to leadership.

25
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:29,360
When something is worth building, one click starts a requirements interview, carrying over what the interviews found.

26
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:38,000
It asks one decision at a time, suggests a sensible default, and pushes back when something does not add up.

27
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Before it finishes, it reads back what it understood, so the owner can correct it.

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:02,000
The result is a versioned specification with numbered requirements, and you can see exactly what changed between versions.

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Download it as a PDF, as Markdown, or as a bundle for coding agents like Claude Code or Codex.

30
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,380
It is built to run on a server you operate, with the AI providers you choose, and every model call is recorded with its cost.

31
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:22,000
Understand the problem, before deciding what to build.
